Title: South Korea to mass produce lasers that can take out drones at $1.50 a hit
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2024/07/11/asia/south-korea-antidrone-lasers-intl-hnk-ml
Content: # South Korea to mass produce lasers that can take out drones at $1.50 a hit

—

South Korea is beginning the mass production of a low-cost laser weapon that has successfully shot down small drones during testing, the country’s key arms agency said Thursd

**********Extracted claims**********

South Korea is beginning the mass production of a low-cost laser weapon.
The low-cost laser weapon has successfully shot down small drones during testing.
The laser weapon is named Block-I.
Block-I can precisely strike small unmanned aerial vehicles and multicopters at close range.
Each shot fired by the Block-I weapon will cost approximately $1.50.
The Block-I unit measures 9 meters by 3 meters by 3 meters (29.5 feet by 9.8 feet by 9.8 feet).
The Block-I weapon fires laser rays that are difficult or impossible to detect before impact.
The Block-I weapon is invisible.
The Block-I weapon is noiseless.
The Block-I weapon does not require separate ammunition.
The Block-I weapon can be operated only when electricity is supplied.
South Korea’s Defense Acquisition Program Administration (DAPA) will develop a laser anti-aircraft weapon system called Block-II.
The Block-II system will have improved output and range compared to the Block-I system.
Small drones have demonstrated the ability to disable or destroy multimillion-dollar military hardware, including tanks.
Militaries have typically responded to low-cost drones with defensive systems costing tens of thousands of dollars per strike.
The Block-I weapon has been in development for five years.
More than $63 million has been invested in the development of the Block-I weapon.
The Korea Institute for Defense Analyses led the development of the Block-I system.
Hanwha Aerospace participated in the development of the Block-I system.
The Block-I system was evaluated as suitable for combat in April 2023.
The Block-I system achieved 100% success in shooting down targets during live-fire tests.
South Korea is the first country to publicly acknowledge it will deploy a mass-produced laser weapon.
Britain has showcased a new laser weapon.
Britain's military claims its new laser weapon could provide lethal missile or aircraft defense.
The cost per shot for Britain's new laser weapon is approximately $13.
No possible deployment date has been announced for Britain's new laser weapon.
The US Navy successfully tested a high-energy laser system in 2022.
The US Navy's 2022 test involved targeting a cruise missile with the laser system.
The US Navy has no plan to deploy its tested high-energy laser system to warfighters.

