Title: Latinos represent nearly a third of new HIV diagnoses in the US, CDC data shows
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2024/07/02/health/latino-new-hiv-diagnosis-rates-reaj
Content: # Latinos represent nearly a third of new HIV diagnoses in the US, CDC data shows

—

When Félix Hernandez learned that he had HIV, he had no one to lean on. In the years since his diagnosis, he found a way to support other people who may be feeling alone

**********Extracted claims**********

Latinos or Hispanic people represent less than 20% of the United States population.
Latinos or Hispanic people accounted for nearly one-third of new HIV diagnoses in the US in 2022.
The latest figures on new HIV diagnoses in 2022 were released by the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC).
Hispanic people have the second highest rate of new HIV diagnoses among all racial and ethnic groups.
There were approximately 19 new HIV diagnoses for every 100,000 Hispanic people.
The rate of new HIV diagnoses for Hispanic people is nearly double the national rate.
New HIV diagnoses among Hispanic people are rising quickly.
New HIV diagnoses overall across the US remained relatively steady between 2018 and 2022.
New HIV diagnoses among Latinos or Hispanic people increased by 19% between 2018 and 2022.
Black people had the highest share of HIV diagnoses in 2022.
New HIV cases among Black people have fallen in recent years.
New HIV cases among Black people decreased by nearly 6% since 2018.
The uninsured rate among Hispanic or Latino people was nearly 18% in 2021.
The uninsured rate among Hispanic or Latino people was among the highest in the nation in 2021 compared to other racial and ethnic groups.
An estimated 8.6% of the overall US population was uninsured in 2021.
The US Census Bureau conducted a survey regarding uninsured rates in 2021.
In 2022, Latino men who have sex with men accounted for more new HIV diagnoses than any other race or ethnicity.
CDC data supports the claim regarding new HIV diagnoses among Latino men who have sex with men in 2022.
Entre Hermanos reports an uptick of HIV cases in the Seattle area in recent years, parallel to the growth of the Latino population there.
A decade ago, medical case managers at Entre Hermanos supported an average of 10 individuals.
The number of clients supported by Entre Hermanos's medical case managers has grown to nearly 200.
Entre Hermanos provides services including HIV testing, HIV medical case management, support groups, and access to pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP).
PrEP is a medication.
PrEP, when used as prescribed, decreases a person’s risk of getting HIV from sex or injection drug use by approximately 99%.
The US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) states the efficacy of PrEP.
A 2021 CDC report stated that awareness of PrEP and referrals to PrEP providers among Latinos tested for HIV at CDC-funded sites in 2019 was low.
Awareness of PrEP and referrals to PrEP providers among Latinos tested for HIV in 2019 was low compared to their White counterparts.
Leandro Ariel Guilamo-Ramos stated that ending the HIV epidemic requires an increased focus on the Latino community.
Guilamo-Ramos presented nine actions to address the HIV epidemic.
The actions proposed by Guilamo-Ramos include the equitable distribution of resources to the Latino community, the elimination of barriers to care, and the building of a "Latino HIV workforce."

