**********Extracted claims**********

Hurricane Beryl has strengthened into a Category 5 Atlantic hurricane.
Hurricane Beryl is the earliest Category 5 Atlantic hurricane on record.
Hurricane Beryl is currently powering across the Caribbean.
Hurricane Beryl brought devastation to the Windward Islands.
At least one person is dead in the Windward Islands due to Hurricane Beryl.
Hurricane Beryl's intensity marks the second time an Atlantic hurricane reached Category 5 status in July.
Hurricane Emily reached Category 5 status on July 17, 2005.
The National Hurricane Center (NHC) provided information on Beryl's intensity and previous Category 5 storms in July.
Hurricane Beryl's maximum sustained winds are near 160 mph.
Hurricane Beryl has gusts higher than 160 mph.
The NHC expects Beryl to remain near major hurricane intensity as it moves into the central Caribbean.
The NHC expects Beryl to pass near Jamaica on Wednesday.
Hurricane Beryl made landfall on Grenada’s Carriacou Island in the Caribbean Sea.
Hurricane Beryl's landfall occurred shortly after 11:00 a.m. EDT.
Hurricane Beryl had maximum winds of 150 mph at landfall.
Hurricane Beryl is the strongest known hurricane to pass through the Grenadines.
Data from NOAA, dating back to 1851, supports Beryl's status as the strongest known hurricane to pass through the Grenadines.
Grenada Prime Minister Dickon Mitchell reported widespread destruction and devastation in Carriacou and Petite Martinique.
Carriacou was flattened in half an hour.
There were no immediate reports of death or injury in Grenada.
At least one death was reported in St. Vincent and the Grenadines.
Hundreds of homes and buildings have been damaged in St. Vincent and the Grenadines.
Prime Minister Ralph Gonsalves announced the damages and fatalities in St. Vincent and the Grenadines.
Parts of St. Vincent and the Grenadines had no water or electricity as of Monday night.
Hurricane Beryl left immense destruction, pain, and suffering across St. Vincent and the Grenadines.
Union Island was devastated.
Reports indicate that 90% of houses on Union Island have been severely damaged or destroyed.
Approximately 95% of Grenada has lost power due to Hurricane Beryl.
Telecommunications across Grenada are down.
Some individuals in Grenada have lost internet service.
All schools and businesses in Grenada are closed.
Grenada's airport is closed.
Only hospitals and the national police force in Grenada are operational.
Grenada's airport reported a sustained wind speed of 92 mph.
Grenada's airport reported a wind gust of 121 mph.
Hurricane Beryl's arrival marks an exceptionally early start to the Atlantic hurricane season.
Hurricane Beryl became the earliest Category 4 hurricane on record in the Atlantic Ocean.
Hurricane Beryl is the only Category 4 hurricane to form in the month of June.
Abnormally warm ocean waters facilitated Hurricane Beryl’s strengthening.
The ocean is currently as warm as it would normally be at the peak of hurricane season.
Hurricane Beryl was located 510 miles east-southeast of Isla Beata in the Dominican Republic as of Monday evening.
Hurricane Beryl had sustained winds of 160 mph as of Monday evening.
Hurricane Beryl was moving to the west-northwest at 22 mph as of Monday evening.
Hurricane Beryl's hurricane-force winds extend 40 miles from its center.
Hurricane Beryl's tropical-storm-force winds extend about 125 miles.
The NHC forecasts Hurricane Beryl’s center to move away from the southern Windward Islands Monday night.
The NHC forecasts Hurricane Beryl’s center to move across the southeastern and central Caribbean Sea through Tuesday.
The NHC forecasts Hurricane Beryl to pass near Jamaica on Wednesday.
The NHC expects hurricane conditions to reach the coast of Jamaica by Wednesday.
Tropical storm warnings are in effect for the south coast of the Dominican Republic from Punta Palenque westward to the border with Haiti.
Tropical storm warnings are in effect for the south coast of Haiti from the border with the Dominican Republic to Anse-d’Hainault.
Over 400 people were housed in hurricane shelters across Barbados on Sunday night.
A state of emergency was declared in Grenada by Governor General Cécile La Grenade on Sunday night.
The state of emergency in Grenada will remain in effect until Tuesday morning.
All businesses in Grenada have closed, with the exception of the police force, hospitals, prisons, waste disposal, and ports.
Airports in Barbados, Grenada, and Saint Lucia were closed on Sunday night.
The Grantley Adams International Airport in Barbados has halted operations.
St. Lucia’s Hewanorra International Airport has halted operations.
St. Lucia’s George Charles Airport has halted operations.
Barbados is hosting cricket fans who traveled for the T20 World Cup.
The hurricane center stated that storm surge could raise water levels by 3 to 5 feet above normal tide levels along the immediate coast of Jamaica in areas of onshore winds.
The hurricane center noted that large swells from Hurricane Beryl will continue in the Windward and southern Leeward Islands for the next couple of days.
The hurricane center expects swells to reach the southern coasts of Puerto Rico and Hispaniola late tonight into Tuesday.
The hurricane center expects these swells to cause life-threatening surf and rip current conditions.
Tropical Storm Chris made landfall near Tuxpan, Mexico.
The landfall of Tropical Storm Chris occurred early Monday.
Hurricane Beryl is the earliest major hurricane in the Atlantic in 58 years.
A major hurricane is defined as Category 3 or higher.
Hurricane Beryl’s rapid intensification is very atypical this early into hurricane season.
National Hurricane Center Director Mike Brennan stated that Beryl's rapid intensification is very atypical this early into hurricane season.
It is rare for tropical systems to form in the central Atlantic east of the Lesser Antilles in June.
Only a handful of tropical systems have formed in the central Atlantic east of the Lesser Antilles in June.
NOAA records support the rarity of such formations.
Hurricane Beryl is the Atlantic Ocean’s third-earliest major hurricane.
Hurricane Alma was the earliest major hurricane in the Atlantic, forming on June 8, 1966.
Hurricane Audrey reached major hurricane status on June 27, 1957.
Hurricane Beryl has set the record for the easternmost hurricane to form in the Tropical Atlantic in June.
A previous record for the easternmost hurricane in the Tropical Atlantic in June was set in 1933.
The central and eastern Atlantic traditionally become more active in August.
Ocean temperatures in the central and eastern Atlantic typically warm by August, fueling developing systems.
The Atlantic basin has seen above-normal water temperatures this year.
The Atlantic basin has seen a lack of wind shear this year.
The lack of wind shear and above-normal water temperatures in the Atlantic basin are due to the transition from El Niño season to La Niña season.
Above-normal water temperatures and a lack of wind shear are fuel for tropical development.
Hurricane Beryl has found an environment with very warm ocean waters for this time of year.
Research from Phil Klotzbach indicates that systems forming early in the summer in this part of the Atlantic are a sign of a hyperactive hurricane season to come.
Ocean temperatures are normally not warm enough in June and July for tropical systems to thrive.
National Weather Service forecasters predict 17 to 25 named storms this hurricane season.
National Weather Service forecasters predict that as many as 13 named storms will become hurricanes this season.
National Weather Service forecaster predictions are well above average, according to Mike Brennan.

