Title: The US held off sanctioning this Israeli army unit despite evidence of abuses. Now its forces are shaping the fight in Gaza
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2024/07/13/middleeast/israel-gaza-netzah-yehuda-human-rights-abuses-investigation-intl
Content: # The US held off sanctioning this Israeli army unit despite evidence of abuses. Now its forces are shaping the fight in Gaza

—

Former commanders of the Netzah Yehuda battalion, an Israeli military unit that has been accused by the United States of gros

**********Extracted claims**********

A CNN investigation found that former commanders of the Netzah Yehuda battalion have been promoted to senior positions in the Israel Defense Forces (IDF).
Former commanders of the Netzah Yehuda battalion are active in training Israeli ground troops.
Former commanders of the Netzah Yehuda battalion are running operations in Gaza.
A CNN investigation included whistleblower testimony from a former soldier of the Netzah Yehuda unit.
The whistleblower described a command within the Netzah Yehuda unit that encouraged a culture of violence.
US State Department investigations identified a culture of violence within the Netzah Yehuda unit.
In April, the US State Department determined that five Israeli security units committed gross violations of human rights prior to the outbreak of the war with Hamas in Gaza.
The US State Department stated that four of these five units had "effectively remediated" or reformed themselves.
The US State Department was still deciding whether to restrict US military assistance to the Netzah Yehuda battalion.
The Netzah Yehuda battalion was originally created to accommodate ultra-Orthodox Jews in the military.
News of potential US assistance withholding from an Israeli military unit triggered a furious response from top Israeli officials, including Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu.
US Secretary of State Antony Blinken told House Speaker Mike Johnson that the US was working with Israel to identify a path to effective remediation for the Netzah Yehuda battalion.
The letter from US Secretary of State Antony Blinken did not name the unit.
Current and former US officials confirmed that Blinken was referring to Netzah Yehuda.
Netzah Yehuda has been accused of a string of abuses in the Israeli-occupied West Bank over the last 10 years.
Accusations against Netzah Yehuda include involvement in the death of a 78-year-old Palestinian-American man in 2022.
CNN found that three former commanders of the Netzah Yehuda battalion have risen through the ranks of the IDF.
These three former commanders were in charge of Netzah Yehuda at the time of alleged abuses in the West Bank.
A former member of the Netzah Yehuda unit detailed instances of cruel and excessively violent treatment of Palestinians in the Israeli-occupied West Bank.
The whistleblower stated that commanders actively supported vigilante violence.
The whistleblower stated that the Netzah Yehuda unit was well known for carrying out "collective punishment of Palestinians."
The former soldier stated that the battalion’s commanders played a key role in perpetuating a culture of violence by standing by as it happened and promoting it.
The IDF stated that the Netzah Yehuda battalion "operates in a professional and ethical manner."
The IDF stated that its soldiers and commanders "act according to the orders and protocols expected of soldiers in the IDF."
The IDF stated that it investigates "every exceptional incident" and takes command and disciplinary measures against those involved when appropriate.
Current and former US officials revealed intense frustrations within the Biden administration regarding perceived special treatment that Israel receives from the US concerning human rights violations by its security forces.
The US determined that four of the five Israeli units under scrutiny were remediated based on Israel taking steps to bring responsible service members to justice.
Israeli military veterans from Breaking the Silence stated that the IDF often scapegoats junior soldiers or officers.
A US State Department spokesperson stated that experts concluded several Israeli security force units were credibly implicated in gross violations of human rights (GVHRs).
For four of the implicated Israeli security force units, the Israeli government had taken effective steps to bring those responsible to justice.
All US security assistance to Israel is provided consistent with domestic and international law.
The five Israeli units were not the only ones the State Department had been examining.
A special State Department panel reached unanimous consensus that three additional units were guilty of abuses prior to October 7.
Incidents involving the three additional units include the alleged killing of Ahmad Jamil Fahd by the Israeli police counterterrorism unit Yamam near Ramallah in May 2021.
Incidents involving the three additional units include the alleged killing of Bedouin man Sanad Salaam al-Harbad by the Israel Border Police in Rahat in March 2022.
Incidents involving the three additional units include the alleged rape of a 15-year-old boy by an interrogator from the Israeli Internal Security Forces in Jerusalem in January 2021.
Josh Paul stated there was no basis to suggest that the Yamam, the Israel Border Police, and Internal Security Forces connected to the Moscobiyya detention center had reformed.
The Moscobiyya rape allegation was believed to be credible and was raised with Israel’s government.
Two of the units, Yamam and Israel Border Police, have been linked to deadly incidents after October 7.
The Yamam unit was involved in Israel’s hostage rescue operation in Nuseirat refugee camp in northern Gaza on June 8.
The Nuseirat operation freed four Israelis.
Local health authorities state that the Nuseirat operation killed more than 270 Palestinians and injured over 700.
The IDF disputes the casualty numbers from the Nuseirat operation, claiming casualties were "under 100."
The Israel Border Police shot and killed a 3-year-old Palestinian girl in the occupied West Bank in January.
The Israel Border Police shot and killed a 12-year-old Palestinian boy in occupied East Jerusalem in March.
The US is the largest supplier of weapons to Israel.
US military assistance has helped shape Israel’s operations in Gaza.
Hamas militants killed approximately 1,200 Israelis and kidnapped more than 250 people in October 2023.
Israel’s assault on Gaza has killed more than 38,000 Palestinians, according to local health authorities.
US President Joe Biden has called for the war to end.
The Biden administration has continued to supply Israel with billions of dollars’ worth of weapons.
The Leahy law, authored by former Sen. Patrick Leahy, was enacted in 1997.
The Leahy law prohibits the US from providing assistance to any foreign security units credibly implicated in human rights violations.
Under the Leahy law, the State Department vets both the unit and its commander when assistance is designated for an entire unit.
The Netzah Yehuda battalion was created by the Israeli military in 1999 for ultra-Orthodox Jews.
The Netzah Yehuda battalion was created to accommodate stringent religious practices like the separation of men and women.
The Netzah Yehuda battalion has attracted religious nationalists from the West Bank settler movement.
The Netzah Yehuda battalion forms part of the Kfir Brigade.
The Kfir Brigade is the largest infantry brigade in the IDF.
A widely reported incident involving the Netzah Yehuda battalion was the death of 78-year-old Palestinian-American man Omar Assad in January 2022.
Omar Assad was detained in his home village of Jiljilya in the occupied West Bank.
Omar Assad was held gagged and with his hands tied by Netzah Yehuda soldiers.
Netzah Yehuda soldiers left Omar Assad unresponsive before his death.
An autopsy report determined that Omar Assad died from a heart attack after he was detained.
An IDF investigation concluded that Omar Assad's death resulted from "a moral failure and poor decision-making on the part of the soldiers."
The IDF reprimanded Netzah Yehuda commander Lt. Col. Mati Shevach after the Assad incident.
The IDF removed the platoon commander and company commander from their positions for two years after the Assad incident.
No criminal charges were brought against the soldiers involved in the Assad incident.
The military stated there was no causal link found between Assad’s death and the soldiers' conduct.
Current and former US officials stated that the Assad case reflects a broader trend of cases examined by the US.
Charles Blaha stated that the State Department has never made an independent determination of a gross violation of human rights by an Israeli unit.
Lt. Col. Mati Shevach was promoted to deputy commander of the Kfir Brigade after his command of Netzah Yehuda ended in August 2022.
Lt. Col. Mati Shevach is training soldiers who are about to enter combat.
Lt. Col. Mati Shevach is running drills for Israeli forces at the military’s Urban Warfare Training Center in Tze’elim military base.
Two other commanders who oversaw Netzah Yehuda during alleged abuses in the occupied West Bank were also promoted.
Lt. Col. Nitai Okashi oversaw the Netzah Yehuda battalion from 2018 to 2020.
In January 2019, Netzah Yehuda soldiers were captured on video beating and taunting a Palestinian father and son after their arrest in the occupied West Bank.
Four Netzah Yehuda soldiers were later convicted of aggravated abuse for the January 2019 incident.
In October 2019, 14 Netzah Yehuda soldiers were arrested for assaulting Bedouin men at a gas station in the West Bank.
Lt. Col. Nitai Okashi has been promoted into a number of commanding roles in the IDF since leaving the battalion.
Lt. Col. Nitai Okashi has operated in Gaza as the commander of the Jerusalem Brigade since the beginning of the war.
The IDF announced a further promotion for Lt. Col. Nitai Okashi in March.
Lt. Col. Uri Levy was in charge of Netzah Yehuda from 2014 to 2016.
In October 2015, a Netzah Yehuda soldier was indicted for allegedly administering electric shocks to Palestinian suspects in two separate incidents.
Lt. Col. Uri Levy was promoted to work in the Kfir Brigade after leaving Netzah Yehuda.
Lt. Col. Uri Levy retired from the military in 2023.
Lt. Col. Uri Levy regularly appears on Israeli talk shows as a pundit.
The IDF stated that soldiers involved in the 2015 and 2019 events had been indicted, and military courts had imposed prison sentences along with additional punishments.
Netzah Yehuda had been stationed in the occupied West Bank since its inception.
In late 2022, Netzah Yehuda was reassigned to the Israeli-controlled Golan Heights.
The IDF stated the reassignment was an extended operational deployment.
US Secretary of State Antony Blinken wrote that the reassignment was an acknowledgment that the battalion had "engaged in conduct inconsistent with IDF rules."
Netzah Yehuda soldiers have been fighting in Gaza since the start of the war.
On April 16, under the leadership of Lt. Col. Shlomo Shiran, Netzah Yehuda was involved in an operation at the Mahdiyya Al-Shawwa school in Beit Hanoun, northern Gaza.
Thousands of displaced Palestinians were sheltering at the Mahdiyya Al-Shawwa school.
Eyewitnesses stated that soldiers surrounded the school and "fired excessively" on the complex.
Eyewitnesses stated that soldiers forced men to strip naked before detaining them at the school.
The IDF stated that intelligence indicated Hamas fighters were in the area of the Mahdiyya Al-Shawwa school.
A voice note allegedly recorded by Palestinians inside the school captured panic and gunshots as the attack unfolded.
A video posted on social media showed a Palestinian man forced to strip naked in front of an IDF tank.
A former Netzah Yehuda soldier stated that he heard about soldiers being rewarded with two weeks off for killing a terrorist.
The IDF stated that the claim of soldiers being rewarded with early leave for killing terrorists was "false and baseless."

**********Claims for hard query**********

US President Joe Biden has called for the war to end.
Thousands of displaced Palestinians were sheltering at the Mahdiyya Al-Shawwa school.
The Leahy law, authored by former Sen. Patrick Leahy, was enacted in 1997.

**********Formulated hard query**********

*   The US held off sanctioning `a certain unit` despite evidence of abuses.
*   Former commanders of `a certain battalion` have been promoted to senior positions in the Israel Defense Forces (IDF).
*   Among `certain findings` was rare whistleblower testimony from a former soldier of the unit.
*   In April, `a certain department` said that it had determined five Israeli security units had committed gross violations of human rights prior to the outbreak of the war with Hamas in Gaza.
*   The department said that four of the units had `in a particular manner`, or reformed themselves, in the wake of those violations.
*   The Netzah Yehuda battalion was originally created to accommodate `certain people` in the military.
*   The news that the US might withhold assistance from the Israeli military unit triggered a furious response at the time from top Israeli officials, including `someone`.
*   In a letter obtained by CNN, `a certain official` told House Speaker Mike Johnson that the US was working with Israel “on identifying a path to effective remediation” for the Netzah Yehuda battalion.
*   Using `a certain technology` and other open-source techniques, CNN has found that three former commanders of the Netzah Yehuda battalion – who were in charge of the unit at the time of alleged abuses in the West Bank – have risen through the ranks of the IDF.
*   CNN has spoken with a former member of the unit, who detailed instances of `in a certain state` of Palestinians in the occupied West Bank.
*   The whistleblower said that commanders actively supported `a certain action` and that promoting them into senior IDF positions risked bringing the same culture to other parts of the military.
*   The former soldier, who asked not to be named due to fears about his security, told CNN that the unit was well known for carrying out what he described as `a certain process`.
