\- Source:

Video 

\- Sou

**********Extracted claims**********

New yen notes were stocked in ATMs across Japan.
The new yen notes are sourced from vibrant yellow flowering paperbush shrubs.
These shrubs grow on craggy Himalayan mountains in Nepal.
The yen notes underwent a long and complex journey before reaching Japanese consumers.
This journey involved months of labor.
This journey involved transport by land and air across thousands of kilometers.
The process of sourcing paperbush for yen notes offers a potential new source of income to communities in Nepal.
Nepal is described as one of the world's poorest countries.
Japan is described as one of the world's richest countries.
Japan has promoted increased digital payments in recent years.
Cash remains the primary payment method in Japan.
Japan lags behind other Asian countries, such as China, in cashless transactions.
China has become almost completely cashless.
Tadashi Matsubara, president of Kanpou, believes Nepal contributed to Japan’s economy.
Tadashi Matsubara, president of Kanpou, believes cash is fundamental to the Japanese economy.
Kanpou is the company that produces paper for the Japanese government.
Tadashi Matsubara believes Japan would not function without Nepal.
The process of producing yen notes from shrubs begins in Nepal at the foot of the Himalayas.
These areas in Nepal are near towns known as gateways to Mount Everest.
These towns have historically been known for reasons other than agriculture.
Mitsumata plants, also known as argeli or paperbush, flower in yellow on hillsides every spring in Nepal.
Mitsumata plants are native to the Himalayan range.
The bark of the mitsumata plant has long, strong fibers.
These fibers are suitable for making thin yet durable paper, according to the Kanpou website.
Mitsumata was previously grown domestically in Japan.
Domestic production of mitsumata in Japan has been slowly decreasing for years.
Producing mitsumata is considered hard work associated with the countryside.
People in Japan are increasingly relocating from rural areas to large cities like Tokyo to find employment.
This rural-to-urban migration results in shrinking villages and declining industries in Japan.
Tadashi Matsubara stated that the number of farmers producing paperbush is decreasing.
The diminishing rural population in Japan is exacerbated by Japan’s demographic crisis.
Japan's demographic crisis includes plummeting birth rates.
Tadashi Matsubara stated that there are no inheritors for paperbush farms due to the demographic situation.
Kanpou first visited Nepal in the 1990s as part of a charity program to assist farmers with well-digging.
During their visit, Kanpou discovered paperbush growing extensively on Nepali mountains.
Kanpou started instructing Nepali farmers on how to cultivate paperbush.
Initially, paperbush from Nepal was produced and exported in small amounts.
A shortage of Japanese paperbush became apparent in subsequent years.
Kanpou and Nepali farmers increased paperbush production.
Kanpou and Nepali farmers became the primary source for the yen bill's paper.
Tadashi Matsubara described the paperbush production process as protracted.
Farmers plant paperbush seedlings in early summer.
Farmers harvest paperbush branches in the fall.
Farmers spend several months processing the bark by steaming, peeling, washing, and drying it.
Raw paperbush material is ready in the winter.
The raw paper is sent to Kathmandu, Nepal.
The raw paper is driven from Kathmandu to Kolkata, India.
The raw paper is transported by ship from Kolkata to Yokohama, Japan.
After inspection, the paper is further processed by the National Printing Bureau.
The paper is printed by the National Printing Bureau.
The paper is cut into cash by the National Printing Bureau.
The National Printing Bureau is located in Odawara city, Japan.
The new yen bills have several new features, according to the Bank of Japan.
These new features include hologram portraits.
The hologram portraits depict prominent historical figures.
The purpose of the hologram portraits is to prevent counterfeiting.
The portrait heads in the holograms appear to turn when the bill is moved.
Other countries have used holograms on currency previously.
The use of hologram portraits on the new yen bills is a first, according to the central bank.
Other features of the new yen bills include parts printed in pearl ink.
Other features of the new yen bills include parts printed in luminescent ink.
Other features of the new yen bills include tactile marks for the visually impaired.
Demand for paperbush is increasing as new yen bills circulate.
Tadashi Matsubara stated that the new notes seem to use more raw material than the older notes.
In 2022, paper articles and paper scraps constituted over 9% of Nepal’s exports to Japan.
The value of these exports was $1.2 million in 2022.
This data is according to the Observatory of Economic Complexity (OEC).
The OEC visualizes and distributes international trade data.
The paper articles and paper scraps exported include products other than the paperbush used for currency.
Over 60% of transactions in Japan were conducted using cash.
The remaining transactions were done via digital payments and other methods.
This information is according to Japan's national Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry.
Tadashi Matsubara stated that profits from paperbush sales have created a new revenue source for Nepali communities.
Tadashi Matsubara claimed the growing paperbush industry has aided in constructing new facilities and infrastructure in Kanpou’s partner villages.
Tadashi Matsubara claimed the growing paperbush industry has provided newfound financial stability to vulnerable families.
Kanpou has received aid funding from Japan’s government’s Japan International Cooperation Agency since 2016.
This funding has enabled Kanpou to expand its operations, according to Tadashi Matsubara.
Kanpou lacks data on the average incomes for its partner villages.
Tadashi Matsubara estimated that each household in these villages earns less than 10,000 yen (approximately $62).
This estimation is based on the absence of other prominent agricultural products at that altitude.
In 2015, the average monthly income for rural Nepali households was 27,511 Nepalese rupees (approximately $205).
This data is according to the global economic database CEIC.
The most recent paperbush crop from Nepal’s Ilam district was sold to Japan for over 180,000 yen (approximately $1,114).
This sale resulted in a revenue of about 30,000 yen ($185) for each of the six farmer groups participating within the Ilam district, according to Tadashi Matsubara.
CNN could not independently verify Tadashi Matsubara’s claims.
Tadashi Matsubara stated that the new yen bill could not have been produced without Nepal’s mitsumata (paperbush).

