Title: What happened in France’s shock election, and what comes next?
URL: https://edition.cnn.com/2024/07/08/europe/france-election-what-next-explainer-intl
Content: # What happened in France’s shock election, and what comes next?

Video 
\- Source:
—

On Sunday night, joy: French voters had, once again, kept the far right out of power. On Monday morning, uncertainty: A hung parliament, shaky alliances and the threat 

**********Extracted claims**********

French voters prevented the far right from gaining power on Sunday night.
A hung parliament, shaky alliances, and the threat of turbulent years emerged in France on Monday morning.
President Emmanuel Macron called France’s snap parliamentary election to clarify the political situation.
After the shock second-round results, French politics became more muddied than it had been in decades.
A surge in support for the left-wing New Popular Front (NFP) coalition foiled Marine Le Pen’s far-right National Rally (RN) party.
French politics is more disordered after the vote than it was before.
The RN led the first round of voting.
The RN was close to forming France’s first far-right government since the collaborationist Vichy regime of World War II.
More than 200 left-wing and centrist candidates withdrew from the second round of the French election.
The withdrawal of candidates aimed to avoid splitting the vote.
The NFP emerged with the most seats in the decisive second round.
The NFP is a coalition of several parties ranging from the extreme left to the more moderate.
The NFP won 182 seats in the National Assembly.
The National Assembly is a 577-seat parliament.
The NFP is the largest group in the 577-seat parliament.
Macron’s centrist Ensemble alliance won 163 seats.
The RN and its allies won 143 seats.
The NFP coalition has the most seats in parliament.
The NFP fell short of the 289 seats required for an absolute majority.
France now has a hung parliament.
The "cordon sanitaire" is a principle stating that mainstream parties must unite to prevent the extreme right from taking office.
Jordan Bardella is the 28-year-old leader of the RN.
Marine Le Pen handpicked Jordan Bardella to refresh the RN's image and purge it of its racist and antisemitic roots.
Jordan Bardella criticized electoral deals made between the NFP and Ensemble.
Jordan Bardella stated that Emmanuel Macron has pushed France towards uncertainty and instability.
Jordan Bardella dismissed the NFP as an "alliance of dishonor."
The RN won eight seats in the 2017 elections.
The RN won 89 seats in 2022.
The RN won 125 seats in Sunday’s vote.
The RN is the largest individual party in the French parliament.
The NFP did not exist a month prior to the election.
The NFP is now the largest bloc in the French parliament.
The NFP chose its name to resurrect the original Popular Front.
The original Popular Front blocked the far right from gaining power in 1936.
The NFP achieved its founding purpose of blocking the far right.
The NFP bloc comprises the far-left France Unbowed party, the Socialists, the green Ecologists, and the center-left Place Publique, among others.
Each party within the NFP celebrated results at their own campaign events.
Jean-Luc Mélenchon is the populist leader of France Unbowed.
Raphael Glucksmann is the more moderate leader of Place Publique.
Jean-Luc Mélenchon and Raphael Glucksmann are barely on speaking terms.
The NFP’s spending plans include raising the minimum wage.
The NFP’s spending plans include capping the price of certain foods and energy.
The NFP’s spending plans include scrapping Macron’s pension reforms.
The European Union has restrictive fiscal rules.
France needs to control its ballooning deficit.
Emmanuel Macron called a snap election three years earlier than necessary.
Macron’s party was significantly behind in the polls when he called the snap election.
Macron’s decision to call the election surprised his closest allies.
Macron called the vote shortly after his party was defeated by the RN in the previous month’s European Parliament elections.
Macron stated he could not ignore the message sent by voters.
Macron stated he wanted to clarify the political situation.
Éduoard Philippe is France’s former prime minister and an ally of Macron.
Éduoard Philippe stated that what was intended as a clarification instead led to great vagueness.
Macron’s party recovered from the first round.
Macron’s party lost around 100 seats compared to the 2022 election.
Macron’s first decision is to appoint a new prime minister.
Macron has delayed the process of appointing a new prime minister.
Macron declined Gabriel Attal’s resignation.
Macron asked Gabriel Attal to remain in office.
The French president typically appoints a prime minister from the largest bloc in parliament.
Mélenchon’s party won the most seats within the NFP.
Macron’s allies have repeatedly refused to work with France Unbowed.
Macron’s allies state France Unbowed is as extreme as the RN.
Macron’s allies state France Unbowed is unfit to govern.
The NFP will likely need to enter into alliances with Ensemble to achieve the majority required to pass laws.
Without a clear majority, a minority government risks no-confidence votes as early as this month.
A lack of clear majority could lead to multiple governments succeeding each other.
A "technocratic" government involves the president appointing ministers with no party affiliation to manage day-to-day matters.
Mario Draghi’s premiership in Italy was a technocratic government.
After Mario Draghi's premiership, Italy elected its most far-right government since Benito Mussolini.
France avoided a far-right government for the time being.
The RN threat is likely to remain strong.

